## The finds worth knowing about

**`drift_report` was computing two ids and dropping them.** It called
`diff_snapshots`, which returns `old_id` and `new_id`, and did not pass them
through — so the diff window could display a comparison it had no way to
export, because the report endpoint builds from snapshots rather than from
diff text the browser happens to be holding. Two lines. Without it, Export
would have worked only after a config push, and the failure would have
looked like a UI bug rather than a missing field.

**Markdown and HTML render from one block list, not one converted into the
other.** Converting means parsing Markdown that contains arbitrary device
output, and device output is angle brackets, ampersands, backticks and lines
starting with a hash. A Cisco banner carrying three backticks closes a code
fence early — it raises nothing and silently reformats the rest of the
report as prose.

**The Jira mask guard covered only one of three sources.** Eight bullet
characters reaching the vault by any other route — the settings API is
scriptable — would have been sent to Jira as the token. That fails much
later, as "Jira rejected ShellMate", with nothing connecting it to the edit
that caused it. Found by `test_ticketing.py` on its first run.

**Three of my own test assertions were wrong rather than the code**, and all
three would have passed for the wrong reason: "no `http` anywhere" flagged
xterm.js's own licence-header attribution URLs (a comment is not a request);
"no `**`" flagged the eight-asterisk redaction mask, not Markdown bold; and
a 48 KB fixture cannot exercise a bound whose floor is 100 KB, so the bounds
test was passing by not testing anything.


## Unplanned, and worth reading

The runner session shipped `e35fe6b` mid-run and **`/api/v1/jobs` is now
paginated** — 100 by default, 500 max. `jobs()` returned a bare list with a
docstring saying "every run the runner knows of", which became false. Fixed
in `a14d7d4`: it returns a window that describes itself, and the Runs area
says on screen when there is more. Two bounds, not one — even `total` is
only what the runner has not pruned (about three weeks of an hourly
pipeline), so nothing there claims to be a complete history any more.

They also reported the event-ordering bug as affecting ShellMate. It did
not, and the premise came from something I had told them: `events()` fetches
the whole set, filters across all of it and re-sorts by counter itself, so
it never used the response order as a cursor. Corrected with them; they have
told Steven they overstated it. Their `?since=` is adopted anyway, because
pulling a whole run's events on every poll to discard most is a real cost.


